= Skillsheet B | ECON 0100 | Next offering draft

== How to use this sheet

This sheet lists every assessed skill in Part B. Your grade is the percentage of skills you pass across the semester, so this sheet is the container for your studying: it tells you what each skill is, where we build it, what practice unlocks it, and the standard you must meet to pass it on Checkpoint B.

Each skill has three types of practice: the *Exercise* (done together in class), the *Vignette* (done together in recitation), and the *Homework* (done on your own time, due Sundays). Practice is graded for completion, not correctness. But youwill get feedback on which Homework questions you got right and wrong.

- Complete *2 of 3* practices for a skill to unlock the skill on Checkpoint B.
- Complete *3 of 3* practices for a skill to unlock the Reattempt.

Skills you pass stay passed. If you do not pass a skill on Checkpoint B, complete all three practices and take the Reattempt.


*Assessment scope.* The standards below define the evidence required for each pass. Show calculation setups; minor arithmetic slips do not erase a correct method. Skills are judged separately.

*Planning copy for the next offering; this does not replace the current semester’s published standards.*

== B1.1 | Demand

Demand relates price, quantity demanded, and marginal benefit.

*Practice:* Exercise B1 · Vignette B1 · Homework B1.

*You pass if you can:*

- Draw a linear demand curve from its equation, with price and quantity axes and the vertical intercept.
- Calculate quantity demanded at a given price and marginal benefit at a given quantity.

== B1.2 | Consumer Surplus

Consumer surplus is the benefit buyers retain after payment.

*Practice:* Exercise B1 · Vignette B1 · Homework B1.

*You pass if you can:*

- Identify the consumer-surplus triangle and expenditure rectangle on a demand graph.
- Calculate consumer surplus and expenditure at one given price.


#pagebreak()
== B2.1 | Supply

The height of supply measures marginal cost.

*Practice:* Exercise B2 · Vignette B2 · Homework B2.

*You pass if you can:*

- Draw a linear supply curve from its equation, with price and quantity axes and the vertical intercept.
- Calculate quantity supplied at a given price and marginal cost at a given quantity.

== B2.2 | Producer Surplus

Producer surplus is revenue above the cost represented by supply.

*Practice:* Exercise B2 · Vignette B2 · Homework B2.

*You pass if you can:*

- Identify the producer-surplus triangle on a supply graph.
- Calculate producer surplus at one given price, distinguishing it from total revenue.

== B3.1 | Equilibrium

Equilibrium equates quantity demanded and quantity supplied.

*Practice:* Exercise B3 · Vignette B3 · Homework B3.

*You pass if you can:*

- Solve for equilibrium price and quantity from linear supply and demand.
- Use quantities at a given price to identify and measure a shortage or excess, and determine the direction of price adjustment.

== B4.1 | Efficiency & Total Surplus

With all costs and benefits captured by the curves, equilibrium maximizes total surplus.

*Practice:* Exercise B4 · Vignette B4 · Homework B4.

*You pass if you can:*

- Compare marginal benefit and marginal cost at a specified quantity to determine whether an additional trade would increase total surplus.
- For a quantity below equilibrium, identify and calculate deadweight loss relative to the efficient quantity, assuming the highest-value buyers and lowest-cost sellers trade.


#pagebreak()
== B4.2 | Price Controls

A binding price floor or ceiling restricts trade.

*Practice:* Exercise B4 · Vignette B4 · Homework B4.

*You pass if you can:*

- Determine whether a given floor or ceiling binds relative to equilibrium.
- Calculate quantity demanded, quantity supplied, and quantity exchanged without government purchases.
- Identify and measure the shortage or excess. No new surplus or DWL calculation is required for this skill.

== B5.1 | Supply & Demand Shifters

Changes in market conditions can shift demand or supply.

*Practice:* Exercise B5 · Vignette B5 · Homework B5.

*You pass if you can:*

- For a stated demand-side event and a stated supply-side event, identify the curve and direction of each shift.
- Draw and label both shifted curves.

== B5.2 | Price Elasticity

Elasticity measures proportional responsiveness, rather than slope.

*Practice:* Exercise B5 · Vignette B5 · Homework B5.

*You pass if you can:*

- Calculate midpoint price elasticity from two price-quantity observations for demand or supply; one calculation is sufficient on an attempt.
- Classify the response as elastic, unit elastic, or inelastic, using the absolute value for demand.

== B5.3 | Comparative Statics

Comparative statics compares equilibria before and after changes.

*Practice:* Exercise B5 · Vignette B5 · Homework B5.

*You pass if you can:*

- When demand and supply both shift, use their directions to identify the determined price or quantity change and the change that depends on their relative sizes. No new algebraic equilibrium calculation is required.

